1
00:00:00,552 --> 00:00:05,168
This is a computer narrated demonstration of CampusDesk, the DevOps capstone repository

2
00:00:05,168 --> 00:00:09,783
of T. Abdul Kalam Azad, roll number 24 B C S 10053.

3
00:00:09,783 --> 00:00:14,399
The voice is synthetic. The application, commands, deployments, and verification results shown

4
00:00:14,399 --> 00:00:19,014
are real. We begin with twelve slides, followed by the working systems.

5
00:00:25,423 --> 00:00:29,526
CampusDesk organizes campus IT support. Students and staff report issues, and the

6
00:00:29,526 --> 00:00:33,628
support team assigns ownership, prioritizes work, and tracks resolution. This project connects

7
00:00:33,628 --> 00:00:37,731
that application to a reproducible DevOps delivery process.

8
00:00:42,769 --> 00:00:46,487
Requests cover network, hardware, software, and account access. The interface provides search,

9
00:00:46,487 --> 00:00:50,205
status filters, priority, assignment, and an editable ticket workflow. All records used

10
00:00:50,205 --> 00:00:53,923
in this demonstration are synthetic.

11
00:01:00,617 --> 00:01:04,976
React and Nginx provide the browser interface. FastAPI validates API requests. SQLAlchemy

12
00:01:04,976 --> 00:01:09,335
talks to PostgreSQL, while Alembic versions the database schema. Forty API tests

13
00:01:09,335 --> 00:01:13,694
cover isolated test databases, and CI repeats them against PostgreSQL.

14
00:01:20,793 --> 00:01:24,447
Traffic enters through Traefik. The frontend and backend use Kubernetes Services. PostgreSQL

15
00:01:24,447 --> 00:01:28,101
keeps ticket data in a persistent volume. GitHub Actions tests and scans

16
00:01:28,101 --> 00:01:31,755
images, then publishes them to GHCR. Argo CD reconciles the Helm configuration

17
00:01:31,755 --> 00:01:35,408
from Git.

18
00:01:40,022 --> 00:01:44,509
The responsive helpdesk is an original CampusDesk implementation. The browser checks verify

19
00:01:44,509 --> 00:01:48,996
real creation, update, persistence, and deletion, together with narrow screen layouts and

20
00:01:48,996 --> 00:01:53,484
accessible controls. Mocked edge cases are separately identified in the reports.

21
00:01:58,769 --> 00:02:02,712
Publication is gated. Source security and tests must pass before image builds.

22
00:02:02,712 --> 00:02:06,654
Native AMD64 and ARM64 jobs scan both images with Trivy, deploy Helm

23
00:02:06,654 --> 00:02:10,596
into Kind, and run seventeen browser checks. Only those exact verified images

24
00:02:10,596 --> 00:02:14,538
become the commit SHA release.

25
00:02:18,922 --> 00:02:23,057
Kubernetes runs two frontend and at least two backend replicas. Readiness checks

26
00:02:23,057 --> 00:02:27,192
database access, while liveness checks the API process. A real load test

27
00:02:27,192 --> 00:02:31,326
completed thirty one thousand two hundred seventy four successful requests and scaled

28
00:02:31,326 --> 00:02:35,461
the backend from two replicas to four.

29
00:02:37,365 --> 00:02:41,853
Argo CD reads this repository. The migration hook runs before the application

30
00:02:41,853 --> 00:02:46,340
rollout. CI promotes the verified image tags in Git, and Argo synchronizes

31
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:50,827
the desired state. Secrets are generated separately and kept outside Git.

32
00:02:54,577 --> 00:02:58,552
Prometheus scrapes each backend replica. Grafana displays availability, request rate, latency, and

33
00:02:58,552 --> 00:03:02,526
response codes. The dashboard is populated by real requests. Monitoring makes the

34
00:03:02,526 --> 00:03:06,501
effect of traffic and rollout changes visible.

35
00:03:11,939 --> 00:03:15,862
Terraform created a VPC in Mumbai with two public and two private

36
00:03:15,862 --> 00:03:19,785
subnets, a NAT gateway, EKS version one point thirty five, and two

37
00:03:19,785 --> 00:03:23,708
private worker nodes. The initial plan contained sixty one resources. A DNS

38
00:03:23,708 --> 00:03:27,631
interruption was recovered using the existing state, and the follow up plan

39
00:03:27,631 --> 00:03:31,554
found no drift.

40
00:03:33,732 --> 00:03:37,290
The troubleshooting exercises deliberately use an invalid image, a wrong Service selector,

41
00:03:37,290 --> 00:03:40,847
and an invalid readiness path. The evidence shows the failure, diagnosis, repair,

42
00:03:40,847 --> 00:03:44,405
and cleanup. These exercises use disposable resources and keep the actual application

43
00:03:44,405 --> 00:03:47,963
available.

44
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:57,935
Verification includes cleanup. Kubernetes created load balancers and persistent volumes must be

45
00:03:57,935 --> 00:04:02,069
removed before Terraform destroys the cluster and network. The evidence index separates

46
00:04:02,069 --> 00:04:06,204
current CampusDesk results from historical examples. The classroom app still needs authentication,

47
00:04:06,204 --> 00:04:10,339
TLS, and database resilience before production use.

48
00:04:17,661 --> 00:04:22,276
The repository keeps the API, frontend, chart, workflow, and infrastructure together. This

49
00:04:22,276 --> 00:04:26,891
is an excerpt from the actual API source. Validation and database handling

50
00:04:26,891 --> 00:04:31,507
live in the application, while the delivery scripts make its deployment repeatable.

51
00:04:35,366 --> 00:04:39,597
The repository separates backend, frontend, Helm, Kubernetes, Terraform, GitOps, monitoring, and delivery

52
00:04:39,597 --> 00:04:43,828
scripts. These are the real API route declarations: health, readiness, metrics, statistics,

53
00:04:43,828 --> 00:04:48,059
and ticket list, read, create, update, and delete. A commit SHA connects

54
00:04:48,059 --> 00:04:52,289
this source to the exact published container release.

55
00:04:59,735 --> 00:05:03,581
Alembic creates the tickets schema before the application starts. The tests use

56
00:05:03,581 --> 00:05:07,427
a dedicated database and a transaction per test. The dependency override supplies

57
00:05:07,427 --> 00:05:11,273
the test session, and rollback removes the test changes. CI exercises the

58
00:05:11,273 --> 00:05:15,119
same migration on PostgreSQL.

59
00:05:18,523 --> 00:05:22,754
The backend installs dependencies and runs as user ten thousand and one.

60
00:05:22,754 --> 00:05:26,984
The frontend uses a Node build stage and copies only the compiled

61
00:05:26,984 --> 00:05:31,215
site into unprivileged Nginx, running as user one hundred and one. Trivy

62
00:05:31,215 --> 00:05:35,446
scans both runtime images before CI publishes them.

63
00:05:37,422 --> 00:05:41,653
We are now using the running Kubernetes application through Ingress. This ticket

64
00:05:41,653 --> 00:05:45,884
is created through the real API, assigned to campus support, marked resolved,

65
00:05:45,884 --> 00:05:50,115
and deleted. The database and interface are responding live.

66
00:05:53,037 --> 00:05:57,652
The API tests now execute again in an isolated local database. The

67
00:05:57,652 --> 00:06:02,267
CI run additionally verifies PostgreSQL migration upgrade, schema consistency, and downgrade. The

68
00:06:02,267 --> 00:06:06,883
test output shown here is produced by this command during the recording.

